messages:
Lina Park: Quick question — after yesterday's autoscaler change we saw some Deployments terminate pods out-of-order and preStop hooks getting cut off by SIGKILL. Anyone else hit this? Thinking it's the combination of low terminationGracePeriodSeconds + aggressive scale-down. Planning to submit a small Terraform tweak to the k8s_deployment resource. TL;DR: want a quick review before I push to staging.

Pranav Sinha: Can you paste the Deployment snippet (strategy + terminationGracePeriodSeconds) and the TF resource you're planning to update? Also which k8s minor version are we on in staging?

Lina Park: sure — staging is 1.25.x. here's the current manifest snippet (from our kustomize overlays):

```yaml
strategy:
  type: RollingUpdate
  rollingUpdate:
    maxSurge: 1
    maxUnavailable: 25%
terminationGracePeriodSeconds: 30
lifecycle:
  preStop:
    exec:
      command: ["/bin/sh", "-c", "/usr/local/bin/drain-connections.sh && sleep 5"]
```

Lina Park: And the TF patch I'm proposing (k8s_deployment):

```hcl
resource "kubernetes_deployment" "api" {
  metadata { name = "api" }
  spec {
    strategy {
      rolling_update {
        max_surge = 0
        max_unavailable = 1
      }
    }
    template {
      spec {
        termination_grace_period_seconds = 60
        lifecycle {
          pre_stop {
            exec { command = ["/bin/sh","-c","/usr/local/bin/drain-connections.sh && sleep 5"] }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Alex Chen: note: switching max_surge -> 0 + max_unavailable -> 1 is fine for scale-down behavior, but it changes how rolling updates proceed. With max_surge=0 you won't create extras during an update, so updates may be slower. The SIGKILL cutoff is usually when terminationGracePeriodSeconds < preStop+drain duration. Increasing TGP to 60 makes sense. Also ensure the drain script exits quickly if connections already closed.

Maya Ruiz: I hit something similar a month ago — our preStop called a healthcheck-y script that sometimes hung on stale sockets. Two quick ops tips: 1) make drain-connections.sh idempotent + timeout (e.g., 10s), 2) add a probe to mark pod NotReady earlier so loadbalancer stops sending new traffic before preStop runs.

Lina Park: good calls. I'll update the script to forcibly close long-lived sockets after 10s and bump termination_grace_period_seconds to 60 in TF. One question — should we prefer percentage for max_unavailable (e.g., 10%) or absolute 1? This deployment is usually 6 replicas in prod, 3 in staging.

Pranav Sinha: use absolute 1 for staging (safer and predictable). For prod consider 10% or 1 depending on SLA — if 6 replicas, 10% rounds down to 0 (k8s floors), so 1 is safer.

Lina Park: makes sense. I'll set staging -> 1, prod -> 1 as well for now and we can relax later. I'll send a PR with the TF change + updated script. tagging @Alex Chen @Maya Ruiz for final eyeballs. :thumbsup:

infra-bot: terraform plan applied in staging: changed 3 resources (kubernetes_deployment.api) — plan shows termination_grace_period_seconds: 30 -> 60; strategy.rolling_update.max_surge: 1 -> 0; strategy.rolling_update.max_unavailable: 25% -> 1. No deletes.

Pranav Sinha: Looks good to me. When you open the PR, include a short canary runbook: how to verify connections close gracefully and how to rollback (revert TF) if latency spikes.

Lina Park: will do, thanks everyone — will follow up in PR with logs from the staging canary run.
